// v0.3-M4 Phase 0 — R5 composed-suspension scenario (SEED for Phase 2's build-blocking hostile fixture).
//
// STATUS: design template. This does NOT compile yet — `channel<T>()` and the `background`
// handle-form (`let h = background ...`, `.send()`/`.receive()`) are built in Phase 1 / Phase 2.
// It is persisted verbatim per the spike discipline: the P0 spike proved this scenario is
// deadlock-free through the real compiler (via a throwaway sentinel-sleep driver + throwaway
// runtime shims that were torn down); Phase 2 grows THIS file into a real
// `crates/ynz-codegen/tests/` fixture once the channel + handle syntax exists, so the exact
// composed interaction the spike verified becomes a permanent build-blocking gate.
//
// THE SCENARIO (R5): a `background`-spawned CHILD suspends on `channel.send()`-on-full WHILE the
// PARENT polls `channel.receive()` on its handle. Capacity-1 channel forces the backpressure.
// The child's second send can only complete AFTER the parent receives the first value (freeing
// capacity) — that ordering is the composed-suspension proof. If the substrate deadlocked, the
// program would hang; it must instead run to completion with the receiver observing BOTH values.
//
// P0 SPIKE RESULT (through `ynz run`, throwaway driver): S1/S2/S3 all GREEN.
//   received_sum = 30 (both values delivered), parent suspended twice (forwarded-waker wake
//   cycles), child suspended on send-on-full, ordering held (send#2 completed after recv#1),
//   elapsed ~26ms, clean exit — no deadlock, no blocking call, no trap door.
//
// EXPECTED OUTPUT when this becomes a real fixture: the two received values (10, 20) and a final
// "done" — the parent must observe both, proving the child's backpressured send made progress
// only after the parent received.

function producer(lend out: channel<int>) -> nothing {
  // First send fills the capacity-1 channel.
  out.send(10)
  // Second send finds the channel FULL — the task SUSPENDS here (backpressure), and stays
  // suspended until the parent's receive() below frees capacity. This is R5's exact scenario:
  // a child suspended on send-on-full while the parent polls receive.
  out.send(20)
}

function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  // Capacity-1 bounded channel — small enough to force the composed backpressure interaction.
  let ch: channel<int> = channel<int>(1)

  // Spawn the producer as an independent joinable background task, handing it the channel.
  let worker = background producer(ch)

  // The parent receives both values. The first receive frees capacity, which is what lets the
  // child's suspended second send() finally complete — the two endpoints wake each other.
  let first = ch.receive()
  print(first.toString())      // 10
  let second = ch.receive()
  print(second.toString())     // 20

  print(`done`)
}
